## Event, participation and entering law

Authorized calendar: grant January 31, argument March 27 and decision April 24, 2000. Certiorari under section 1254(1) to the Second Circuit, 166 F.3d 507, affirming the district court, 996 F.Supp.343. These are the scheduled review dates; no historical Supreme Court order or docket is invented. All nine participated at argument and decision, with no disqualification supplied; quorum is met and five votes suffice. Research cutoff: April 24, 2000.

The two granted questions concern private commitment under state law and attribution through purported delegation, regulation and enforcement. They do not establish actual official enforcement or decide Fourth/Fourteenth Amendment merits. The record identifies Rosenberg's order and hospital-security transport separately from hospital physicians' certificates and confinement. Conditional restraints were not used. Doe's dangerousness remains disputed. She was confined May 5 and judicially released May 16, 1996. The later release is a separate governmental choice. The federal summary judgment and section 1367(c)(3) pendent-claim dismissal are the judgments under review; the separate discovery ruling is not granted anew.

The frozen neutral and current-term public supplement provide Sullivan's particular-choice rule and Carter's separation of deprivation, attribution, culpability and causation. Powell concerns a different statutory right and private remedy. Blum, Rendell-Baker, Lugar, West and Edmonson retain their actual governmental-connection predicates. No earlier chunk-5 event changes those rules. The statutory source distinguishes section 9.27(a)'s permission, subsection (d)'s alternatives requirement, section 9.01's definitions and the state courts' dangerousness construction. No false attribution of the latter to express subsection (a) language is made.

## Stone

The approved October 31 position requires affirmance on this record. Stone joins the choice-specific distinction between permission/regulation and governmental responsibility, preserving actual coercion, significant encouragement, joint decisionmaking, legal compulsion or delegation of an exclusively governmental function. Medical judgment supplies no blanket immunity. His reservations for commanded, jointly decided, selectively enforced or legally final delegated governmental confinement remain open on their absent predicates. Both transport and confinement receive the approved rationale; no standing fallback supplies a ground or authorship eligibility.

