#### Governing-law comparison

Sullivan's controlling March 3, 1999 attribution holding requires an actual governmental basis for responsibility for the particular private choice. Permission, regulation and public review alone are insufficient. Its payment setting does not automatically decide confinement, so the frozen model separately tests public function, actual delegation and joint participation for Rosenberg's referral/transport and the hospital's certification/confinement. The model preserves West and Edmonson at their governmental-duty and public-machinery predicates.

The current-term public reading supplement was checked for Carter's separate deprivation, attribution, culpability and causation requirements and Powell's distinct statutory-right/private-remedy questions. They were already available to the model and introduce no changed premise. Carter's pleading disposition creates no entitlement to reopen this summary judgment; Powell supplies no answer to attribution of private confinement.

The actual record identifies private physicians and hospital security, not state officers executing the initial confinement. Later judicial release is a different public decision. The established statutory and institutional relationship does not prove a public command, significant encouragement of this choice, joint action or delegation of a traditionally exclusive governmental function. The historical private-confinement evidence was available in neutral preflight and is not newly introduced here. Those predicates support the common result while leaving materially different public custody, governmental contracts or joint decisions open.

#### Every Associate

All eight affirm the attribution ruling for both transport/referral and certification/confinement, preserving federal summary judgment and the pendent-claim dismissal. None adopts the underlying constitutional or state-law merits or a general private-hospital immunity.

| Justice | Justice-specific reconciliation |
|---|---|
| Stevens | His Sullivan reservation declined an unnecessary additional ground, not an affirmative finding of state action. Attribution is now directly granted and dispositive. His Edmonson participation supports the distinction between actual governmental machinery and the present private decisions, with West's delegated-duty limit preserved. |
| O'Connor | Her Sullivan attribution join, with Blum and Rendell-Baker, supports the distinction between regulated permission and responsibility for the challenged act. She retains the specific exceptions for official direction, joint confinement and a materially different governmental undertaking. |
| Scalia | His Sullivan join and Lugar's distinct elements support affirmance without turning severity of deprivation or regulatory density into a substitute attribution test. His position does not decide actual police involvement on absent facts. |
| Kennedy | His Sullivan opinion and Edmonson's actual public-jury setting support the particular-choice analysis. He preserves West where the State has entrusted its custodial duty to a private professional; those facts are not established here. |
| Souter | His Sullivan join supports examining responsibility for the initial decision separately from later review. Neither eventual release nor hypothetical contract terms supply joint participation in this record. |
| Thomas | His Sullivan join preserves the actor requirement independently of state-created permission. The record's function history defeats exclusivity at this scope without deciding every authorized private restraint. |
| Ginsburg | Her Sullivan reservation did not decide attribution affirmatively. Carter expressly preserves the distinction between a deprivation and responsibility for it. She supports the granted attribution ruling while withholding any conclusion that transport is insignificant or dangerousness established. |
| Breyer | His Sullivan join supports examining practical operation and actual governmental involvement. Private certification and hospital-security transport do not become public decisions from licensing and review alone; a proved delegated governmental duty would remain different. |

